.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-STOP-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-stop-application \- stop an application, keeping its definition
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap stop-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Records that this application should not be running, and returns
immediately. The Euclid manager stops every instance of it on its next
reconcile, a few seconds later, by asking each process to terminate -
which is why an application should exit on SIGTERM rather than having to
be killed.
.PP
The definition, the artifact and the identity the application runs as are
all left in place, so
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1)
brings it back as it was. To remove it entirely, use
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-application (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The desired state was recorded. The definition is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, or no application of that name exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap stop-application --application-id inbox
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-application (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
